    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 122010\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n110 PM MST Sat Sep 12 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Abundant moisture will remain situated across the eastern half of \nArizona providing enhanced chances for showers and thunderstorms \nover mountain locations.\n\n- Shower and thunderstorm chances will periodically extend into \nlower desert communities of south-central Arizona.\n\n- Near to slightly above normal temperatures are likely through next \nweek.\n \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Monday/...\nSubtropical ridging has been dampened and displaced into northern \nMexico over the past 48 hours as enhanced westerly flow and \ntroughing in the northern jet stream descend into southern Utah. \nOver the next 60 hours, the anti-cyclone center will become \nreestablished over the SE Conus while quasi-stationary positively \ntilted troughing settles over the Pacific NW and Great Basin. With \nthe ridge axis becoming oriented zonally across far southern \nArizona, weak S/SW flow will result over the forecast area yielding \nperiodic variations between modest subsidence and ascent. This flow \npattern will not be sufficient to scour away any moisture with \nconvectively favorable 12-13 g/kg low level mixing ratios being \nmaintained across the CWA. \n\nConvective potential may be somewhat restricted this afternoon and \nevening as synoptic scale subsidence spreads into the region behind a \nshortwave shearing towards the four corners in the northern stream \nwesterly flow. However, given the ample boundary layer moisture, \nisolated storms formed via moist, southwest flow over terrain \nescarpments seems a reasonable outcome. While forecast CinH does not \nappear overwhelming (less than 25 J/kg) and would seemingly support \nadditional storms along resulting outflows, HREF members advertise \nminimal outflow boundaries and little to no additional \nshowers/storms. Regardless, a few evening thunderstorms and \nlingering overnight elevated showers would not be out of the \nquestion given the underlying moisture profiles. \n\nWhile high resolution modeling indicates nearly identical convective \nconditions Sunday with minimal impacts to lower elevations, \nsubsidence mechanisms appear largely absent while MLCape exceeds \n1500 J/kg with almost no CinH. Admittedly, large scale ascent will \nalso be lacking, but any stronger outflow boundary, and/or colliding \nboundaries would be supportive towards deep convective elements. \nThus, it wouldn't be surprising if thunderstorm chances/coverage is \nmore expansive than the mandated NBM output or majority of HREF \nforecasts during the late afternoon and evening. A similar argument \ncan be made regarding storm chances Monday where the NBM has been \nloading higher POPs into the morning hours versus the more likely \nevening time frame. While its conceivable elevated storms affect \nparts of the CWA during the morning and a minority of modeling shows \nlight QPF in the 12-18Z time frame, this outcome is historically not \nas common or likely; and the current forecast is initiated based on \nfrequently misleading NBM/WPC output with forecaster adjustments \nmade where human intervention is allowable. Of better confidence is \nthe temperature forecast over the next few days where readings \nshould fall within a few degrees of the daily normals. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Tuesday through Friday/...\nRain chances will continue across the eastern half of Arizona during \nthe middle of the week as Pacific troughing lingers just to our \nnorth and northwest, and ridging stays centered over the southeast \nConus. This will continue place Arizona in southwest flow providing \nperiods of moisture advection out of northern Mexico. The biggest \nquestion that remains is what day, or days, will represent the best \nchances for more than just scattered showers and isolated storms. \nModels agree plentiful moisture will remain over eastern and central \nArizona through at least the middle of next week with PWATs running \n150-180% of normal and low level mixing ratios anywhere from 11-14 \ng/kg. However, there are uncertainties with the amount of \ninstability due to forecast cloud cover and lack of any obvious \ntrigger mechanisms. Last night's model runs were more optimistic \nwith upper level support, and this will likely be a piece of the \npuzzle needed for an active weather day given the questionable \ninstability and inhibition. \n\nThe NBM is still honing in on early Tuesday as the best potential \nfor showers and thunderstorms across eastern and central Arizona, \nbut confidence in this is still fairly low. It would not be \nsurprising to have any day Tuesday through Thursday to be a good \nstorm day, but it is still too early to pinpoint which day or days. \nWith the upper level support leaning toward being less of an \ninfluence, we may just have to rely on differential heating and/or \nwherever the western edge of the moisture axis sets up. Forecast \ninstability does look to be enough to support some strong storms and \nwith the moisture levels expected, heavy rainfall leading to \nlocalized flooding could also become an issue. Overall, rain chances \nfor next week look quite conditional and PoPs are likely to change. \nForecast temperatures for next week have also been trending upward \nover the past few days with decreasing chances of having lower \ndesert highs dipping below 100 degrees on any day. The latest NBM \nshows highs hovering right around normal to a few degrees above.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1710Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major aviation weather concerns under FEW-SCT mid and high cloud \ndecks can be expected throughout the TAF period. The overall wind \npattern will generally favor diurnal tendencies with periods of \nlight variability likely at times, especially during diurnal \ntransitions. Overall wind speeds will generally be aob 10 kts.\n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nNo major aviation weather concerns under FEW high cirrus cloud decks \ncan be expected throughout the TAF period. Winds will generally be \nout of the southeast at KIPL and out of the south at KBLH. Winds \nspeeds will generally range between 8-14 kts with afternoon gusts in \nthe 20-25 kt range at KBLH. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nSlight chances for showers and thunderstorms can be expected through \nthe weekend with the best chances over the Arizona high terrain. \nChances are expected to increase across the eastern districts early \nnext week. MinRHs through early next week will generally be hover \nclose to 15-25% for the far western districts, while higher values \ncloser to 25-35% will be seen elsewhere. Winds will somewhat follow \ntypical diurnal patterns, but with more of a south southwesterly \npredominant wind across the western districts. Daily afternoon gusts \nof 20-25 mph will be common across the Lower CO River Valley this \nweekend before becoming more widespread early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
